## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # School Days – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Days</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Yesterday Was Wednesday.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize short stories using drawings or pictures. Communication: Explain simple written instructions to peers, using English or Spanish.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can summarize short stories using drawings or pictures (e.g., draws a sun and says "Sunday" to show the story's setting). Can explain simple written instructions to peers, using gestures (e.g., points to the calendar to show, "Today is Monday."). Can clarify simple instructions, using basic gestures (e.g., gives thumbs up to indicate "yes").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Days of the Week" song, pointing to a large calendar poster. Students sing along and point to the days. The teacher then holds up flashcards for "yesterday," "today," and "tomorrow," asking, "What day is today?" "What day was yesterday?" (e.g., "Today is Thursday. Yesterday was Wednesday.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a simple "Time Travel Calendar" drawing (similar to the project but simpler, just 3 boxes for yesterday, today, tomorrow). For "Yesterday," the teacher draws a picture of reading a book and says, "Yesterday was Wednesday. I read a book." For "Today," the teacher draws a picture of teaching and says, "Today is Thursday. I teach English." The teacher uses gestures like pointing backward for "yesterday" and forward for "today." The teacher models pronouncing /w/ in "Wednesday" and /f/ in "Friday" while pointing to these days on the calendar.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to "yesterday" box): "Is this today or yesterday?" (Students respond: "Yesterday!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a drawing of a math class): "What is it? Math or science?" (Students respond: "Math!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "School Days".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated story or a sequence of 3-4 large pictures depicting a child's school week. For example, Picture 1: child drawing on Wednesday. Picture 2: child playing with friends on Thursday. Picture 3: child learning English on Friday.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Can summarize short stories using drawings or pictures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows the first picture and tells a very simple story: "Look! This is Leo. Yesterday was Wednesday. Leo drew a sun! It was sunny." (Teacher points to the drawing activity and the sun).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows the second picture: "Today is Thursday. Leo plays with friends at school." (Teacher points to Leo and friends).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows the third picture: "Tomorrow is Friday. Leo likes English! He will learn English." (Teacher points to Friday and the English class).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher asks students to point to specific pictures based on a simple summary:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to yesterday's picture. What did Leo do yesterday?" (Students point to the drawing picture and say "draw" or "sun").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Point to the picture where Leo has friends." (Students point to the playing picture).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Summarizing activities using "Yesterday was..." and "Today is..." with target vocabulary (days of the week, school activities) and explaining simple visual instructions/information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Picture-Sentence Matching:</b> The teacher displays flashcards of school activities (e.g., math class, science class, playing with friends, English class, drawing). The teacher then shows a simple sentence (e.g., "Yesterday was Wednesday. We had math.") and students have to point to the correct flashcard. They can also use gestures to show the activity.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Substitution Drill: "What was it?"</b> The teacher shows a picture of an activity and says, "Yesterday was [day of the week]. What was it?" Students respond chorally or individually, filling in the activity: "Yesterday was Tuesday. It was science." (Teacher ensures /w/ and /f/ pronunciation when relevant days are used). The teacher circulates, providing feedback and encouraging full sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "My Day Pictures":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of 2-3 simple picture cards representing activities (e.g., a pencil for 'write', a book for 'read', a sun for 'sunny'). Student A picks a card, shows it to Student B, and says, "Yesterday was [day]. I [verb/activity]." or "Today is [day]. I [verb/activity]." Student B listens and responds, "Yes!" or asks "What is it?" if they don't understand, prompting Student A to clarify with gestures or a simpler word (English or Spanish).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "School Days".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative presentation and explanation of the "Time Travel Calendar" project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "Explain My Time Travel Calendar" (21st Century Skills Project):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students retrieve their "Time Travel Calendar" drawings from previous lessons (which have "yesterday," "today," "tomorrow" sections and drawings for activities).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. Student A points to their "Yesterday" drawing on their calendar and says, "Yesterday was [day]. I [drew/had] [activity, e.g., math]." and then points to their "Today" drawing, saying, "Today is [day]. I [drew/have] [activity, e.g., English]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B listens and, if they don't understand an activity, they can ask "What is it?" or point to their own calendar to clarify. Student A uses gestures, or simple English words (or Spanish if needed for clarification) to explain their drawing/activity.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, Student B takes a turn to explain their calendar to Student A.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing, providing support, and encouraging students to use the target language and gestures for mediation. (e.g., "Give thumbs up if you understand!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on students' ability to summarize and explain their "Time Travel Calendar" project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student briefly presents their "Time Travel Calendar" to the teacher or a small group. They will summarize their chosen activities for "yesterday" and "today" using their drawings as a visual aid.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students point to their "Yesterday" drawing and say, "Yesterday was [day]. I [activity]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students point to their "Today" drawing and say, "Today is [day]. I [activity]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They clarify any unclear drawings or words using gestures or simple explanations (English or Spanish).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> Accurately uses "Yesterday was..." and "Today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Uses days of the week, activity words (math, English, science, draw, read, play, friends).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of explanation:</b> Explains drawings clearly using words and gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of /w/ in "Wednesday" and /f/ in "Friday."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I explain my calendar drawings clearly to my friend?" (Thumbs up/down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I use 'Yesterday was' and 'Today is'?" (Yes/No).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy about explaining your 'Time Travel Calendar'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you use or learn today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students summarize information and explain their drawings to peers? (e.g., use of gestures, simple sentences).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled to mediate their ideas, even with L1 support? (e.g., more direct modeling, sentence frames).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully integrate the pronunciation of /w/ and /f/ when discussing days?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concluded the "Time Travel Calendar" project and the "School Days" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme and set of vocabulary, building on students' ability to talk about daily routines and time markers.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>